// Trust a fixed newcomer key from a deploy, and publish the op's reply.
//
// The first half of the #203 probe: `trust` is the op jimscarver's #74 reproduction used, because it
// has the fewest preconditions of any native write — the caller need only already be trusted, and a
// genesis validator is trusted by construction. So an ordinary deploy can make a native write with no
// setup, which is what makes it the right op to reproduce "the write is acknowledged and lost".
//
//   tools/devnet.sh deploy pos-trust.rho && tools/devnet.sh deploy pos-trusted.rho
//   tools/devnet.sh query pos-trusted     # the newcomer must appear
//
// **The key is exactly 65 bytes (130 hex characters) and that is load-bearing**: `trust` refuses any
// other length, and a refusal reads exactly like the defect this probe exists to catch. The first
// version of this file carried 134 characters and the deploy failed — which made a broken probe look
// like a reproduced bug until the `[deploy] … FAILED` line was read.
new pos(`rho:rchain:pos`), deployerId(`rho:rchain:deployerId`), ret in {
  pos!("trust", *deployerId,
    "0202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202020202".hexToBytes(),
    *ret) |
  for (@result <- ret) {
    @"pos-trust"!(result)
  }
}
